{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","statement":"The useRef Hook returns an object with a single property called current.","source_span":"The `useRef` Hook returns an object with a single property called `current`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","statement":"Initially, myRef.current will be null.","source_span":"Initially, `myRef.current` will be `null`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","statement":"In the focusing text input example, clicking the button calls inputRef.current.focus() to focus the input.","source_span":"function handleClick() {\n    inputRef.current.focus();\n  }"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","statement":"Hooks must only be called at the top level of a component; useRef cannot be called in a loop, condition, or inside a map() call.","source_span":"This is because **Hooks must only be called at the top-level of your component.** You can't call `useRef` in a loop, in a condition, or inside a `map()` call."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","statement":"A ref callback is a function passed to the ref attribute; React calls it with the DOM node when setting the ref, and calls its returned cleanup function when clearing it.","source_span":"React will call your ref callback with the DOM node when it's time to set the ref, and call the cleanup function returned from the callback when it's time to clear it."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","statement":"The carousel example has three cat images with buttons labeled Neo, Millie, and Bella, each using a separate ref.","source_span":"<button onClick={handleScrollToFirstCat}>\n          Neo\n        </button>\n        <button onClick={handleScrollToSecondCat}>\n          Millie\n        </button>\n        <button onClick={handleScrollToThirdCat}>\n          Bella\n        </button>"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","statement":"The carousel example calls scrollIntoView with options behavior: 'smooth', block: 'nearest', inline: 'center'.","source_span":"scrollIntoView({\n        behavior: 'smooth',\n        block: 'nearest',\n        inline: 'center'\n      });"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","statement":"Refs are like state variables that don't trigger re-renders when you set them, and remain between renders similarly to state.","source_span":"Similarly to state, refs remain between renders. Refs are like state variables that don't trigger re-renders when you set them."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What property does the object returned by useRef have, and what is its initial value when useRef(null) is called?","answer":"It has a single property called `current`, which is initially `null`."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","fact_refs":[2],"question":"In the focusing text input example, what method is called on the ref to focus the input when the button is clicked?","answer":"inputRef.current.focus() is called inside the handleClick function."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Why can't you call useRef inside a map() call when trying to create a list of refs?","answer":"Because Hooks must only be called at the top level of a component, not inside a loop, a condition, or a map() call."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are the names of the three cat images shown in the carousel example in the refs documentation?","answer":"Neo, Millie, and Bella."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What options are passed to scrollIntoView() in the cat carousel refs example?","answer":"{ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'nearest', inline: 'center' }"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_manipulating-the-dom-with-refs","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How do refs differ from state in terms of triggering re-renders?","answer":"Refs persist between renders like state does, but unlike state, setting a ref's value does not trigger a re-render."}
